/**
 * @defgroup EOS EOS Gem
 *
 * @brief The @geos gem implements various nuclear equation-of-state (EOS)
 *        schemes.
 *
 * This includes all the core routines to implement GRMHD code for both
 * [hybrid](@ref hyb_eos) and [tabulated](@ref tab_eos) equations of state.
 * Additionally, we provide a simplified hybrid EOS. This is often referred
 * to as the 'ideal fluid' or 'ideal gas' EOS despite the fact that all of
 * these are ideal fluid EOS. This EOS is primarily only of interest for
 * comparing against known results such as the Balsara tests.
 *
 * # EOS initialization
 *
 * Setting up the EOS using @grhayl consists of two steps. First, a
 * ghl_eos_parameters struct should be declared once per simulation (the
 * specifics of this depend on the infrastructure). For long simulations
 * with checkpoint/restart, reconstruct a fresh struct or first free any live
 * tabulated allocations before reusing its storage. The struct can then be
 * initialized by using one of these functions:
 *
 * - @ref ghl_initialize_simple_eos_functions_and_params
 * - @ref ghl_initialize_hybrid_eos_functions_and_params
 * - @ref ghl_initialize_tabulated_eos_functions_and_params
 *
 * which will properly set up the function pointers and struct parameters
 * for the chosen EOS. Flux, source, and characteristic-speed routines use
 * `ghl_compute_h_and_cs2`; custom dispatch users need replace only that
 * combined callback. The deprecated generic
 * `ghl_calculate_HLLE_fluxes_dirn0/1/2` pointers are never assigned. Simple
 * and hybrid parameter initialization publishes a completed object only on
 * success. Their density atmosphere must be finite
 * and positive. For supported `Gamma > 1`, simple derived energy and entropy
 * minima pair `press_min` with `rho_max`, and maxima pair `press_max` with
 * `rho_min`. Simple maxima use `DBL_MAX` for a zero density floor or the `1e300`
 * pressure-ceiling marker, and zero for a zero pressure ceiling.
 * Hybrid derived minima use `-DBL_MAX` when the density floor is disabled, and
 * hybrid derived maxima use `DBL_MAX` when the density ceiling is disabled.
 * Unused atmosphere composition and temperature are set to zero.
 *
 * Tabulated initialization requires a fresh object with no live owned
 * allocations. On failure it releases candidate allocations and returns an
 * empty object tagged `ghl_eos_tabulated`. In HDF5-enabled builds, call
 * `ghl_tabulated_free_memory` before reinitializing a live table or switching
 * its EOS family; cleanup is repeatable for successful, failed-empty,
 * previously cleaned, and properly zero-initialized objects. Disabled-HDF5
 * initialization allocates nothing, returns an empty object, and requires no
 * cleanup.
 *
 * Hybrid callers provide `neos` gamma values and `neos - 1` strictly
 * increasing positive density breakpoints. The breakpoint pointer may be
 * NULL when `neos` is one.
 *
 * Source clients migrating to this interface must remove calls to the former
 * prototype-only `NRPyEOS_tabulated_get_index_rho` and
 * `NRPyEOS_tabulated_get_index_Ye` names and use the supported interpolation
 * routes instead. `NRPyEOS_tabulate_enthalpy` retains its original `void`
 * function type but warns and stops at the first invalid point without
 * returning a status, leaving that and later entries unchanged; the table
 * loader uses `NRPyEOS_tabulate_enthalpy_checked` to reject invalid derived
 * enthalpy.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup hyb_eos Hybrid EOS
 * @ingroup EOS
 *
 * The hybrid EOS splits the pressure into cold and thermal components, and
 * the behavior of the thermal part is controlled with \f$ \Gamma_\mathrm{th} \f$
 * (see ghl_eos_parameters and @ref ghl_initialize_hybrid_eos for
 * implementation specifics). The EOS also supports piecewise polytropes,
 * which allows the hybrid EOS to better approximate tabulated EOS without
 * the computational expense of the full tabulated approach.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup tab_eos Tabulated EOS
 * @ingroup EOS
 *
 * The tabulated EOS uses a table of computed values for many quantities to
 * define the EOS. This EOS requires many interpolations between table
 * entries, as well as the ability to solve for one table variable when
 * given other elements of the table. This has the benefit of better
 * approximating a given nuclear data table compared to e.g. hybrid EOS,
 * but at significant computational expense. @grhayl provides all the code
 * required to both read, interpolate, and compute variables in the table.
 *
 * The offline `tools/compose/compose_to_grhayl.py` utility can produce this
 * existing StellarCollapse HDF5 schema from one fixed CompOSE-generated
 * SRO(SLy4) SNA table-141 schema. Its production profile is explicitly a
 * physics-changing regularized surrogate: it enforces strict temperature-ray
 * inversion, positive heat capacity and enthalpy, causal derivative/sound-speed
 * identities, chemical identities, and node composition closure. It does not
 * add a native CompOSE backend or change the public C API. Runtime sound-speed
 * cleaning must remain disabled for its already-relativistic output. See
 * `tools/compose/README.md` for the exact policy, limitations, and qualification
 * commands.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup eos_internal Internal EOS Functions
 * @ingroup EOS
 *
 * These are internal functions/attributes used by the @ref EOS
 * routines but should not be called by standard users.
 */
